# Constructor Pattern in Go

Go has no built-in constructors like Java or C++. Instead, Go uses **factory functions** with the naming convention `New<TypeName>`. This pattern provides full control over object initialization.

## Why Go Has No Constructors?

Go intentionally omits constructors:
- Keeps the language simple
- Zero values should be useful whenever possible
- Factory functions are more flexible and explicit

## Basic Factory Function

```go
type Person struct {
    name  string
    age   int
    email string
}

func NewPerson(name string, age int, email string) (*Person, error) {
    if name == "" {
        return nil, errors.New("name cannot be empty")
    }
    if age < 0 || age > 150 {
        return nil, fmt.Errorf("invalid age: %d", age)
    }
    return &Person{name: name, age: age, email: email}, nil
}

p, err := NewPerson("Alice", 30, "alice@example.com")
if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
```

## Options Struct Pattern

```go
type ServerOptions struct {
    Host     string
    Port     int
    MaxConns int
    Timeout  time.Duration
}

func DefaultServerOptions() ServerOptions {
    return ServerOptions{
        Host: "localhost", Port: 8080,
        MaxConns: 100, Timeout: 30 * time.Second,
    }
}
```

## Zero-Value Friendly Design

```go
type Stack struct { items []int }

func (s *Stack) Push(v int) { s.items = append(s.items, v) }
func (s *Stack) Pop() (int, bool) {
    if len(s.items) == 0 { return 0, false }
    n := len(s.items) - 1
    v := s.items[n]
    s.items = s.items[:n]
    return v, true
}

var s Stack // zero value works immediately!
s.Push(42)
```

